WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.200
Series, Theorems and Paradoxes, Season 1, Episode 1

00:05.200 --> 00:09.520
Ever wondered how something can be both true and false at the same time?

00:09.520 --> 00:11.880
Welcome to the world of paradoxes.

00:11.880 --> 00:18.280
Paradoxes, in essence, are mind-bending statements or situations that appear to contradict themselves,

00:18.280 --> 00:20.760
defying our intuition and logic.

00:20.760 --> 00:25.180
They exist in a realm where the usual rules don't seem to apply, and they challenge us

00:25.180 --> 00:27.600
to think outside the box.

00:27.600 --> 00:33.080
Take, for instance, the liar paradox, a classic example that's as simple as it is perplexing.

00:33.080 --> 00:34.240
It goes like this.

00:34.240 --> 00:35.680
This statement is false.

00:35.680 --> 00:38.600
Now, is that statement true or false?

00:38.600 --> 00:41.800
If it's true, then it must be false as it declares.

00:41.800 --> 00:47.140
But if it's false, then it's actually telling the truth, which means it's true.

00:47.140 --> 00:51.000
And there you have it, a paradox, a riddle that twists our understanding of truth and

00:51.000 --> 00:54.160
falsity, leaving us in a delightful quandary.

00:54.160 --> 00:59.400
As you can see, paradoxes challenge our understanding of truth and falsity, making us rethink our

00:59.400 --> 01:00.400
assumptions.

01:00.400 --> 01:06.280
Now, imagine if a statement could always be proven true, no matter what.

01:06.280 --> 01:08.360
That's the magic of theorems.

01:08.360 --> 01:11.320
Theorems, you see, are the stars of mathematics and logic.

01:11.320 --> 01:15.560
They're statements that can be proven true based on certain accepted truths known as

01:15.560 --> 01:16.940
axioms.

01:16.940 --> 01:22.160
These axioms are the bedrock, the foundation upon which these powerful truths, the theorems,

01:22.160 --> 01:23.840
are built.

01:23.840 --> 01:28.760
Take, for instance, a famous theorem that you may remember from your school days, Pythagoras's

01:28.760 --> 01:29.760
theorem.

01:29.760 --> 01:32.280
It's a perfect example of this concept.

01:32.280 --> 01:36.560
Stated in simple terms, Pythagoras's theorem proves the relationship between the sides

01:36.560 --> 01:38.560
of a right-angled triangle.

01:38.560 --> 01:43.120
The square of the longest side, the hypotenuse, is always equal to the sum of the squares

01:43.120 --> 01:45.260
of the other two sides.

01:45.260 --> 01:49.440
No matter the size or scale of the triangle, this theorem holds true.

01:49.440 --> 01:55.800
Unlike paradoxes, theorems provide certainty, a rock-solid truth we can rely on.

01:55.800 --> 02:01.360
What if we told you that paradoxes and theorems aren't as separate as they seem?

02:01.360 --> 02:06.640
Let's dive into the intriguing intersection of paradoxes and theorems, a realm where certainty

02:06.640 --> 02:11.940
clashes with uncertainty, creating a mesmerizing dance of the intellect.

02:11.940 --> 02:16.720
One might assume that theorems, the bedrock of mathematical and logical truth, would be

02:16.720 --> 02:20.000
immune to the bewildering contradictions of paradoxes.

02:20.000 --> 02:22.500
However, this is far from reality.

02:22.500 --> 02:29.300
Take, for instance, Gödel's incompleteness theorems, a remarkable example of this intersection.

02:29.300 --> 02:33.520
In a nutshell, these theorems introduced by the mathematician Kurt Gödel in the early

02:33.520 --> 02:38.280
20th century shake the very foundations of mathematics.

02:38.280 --> 02:43.180
They assert that within any given system of rules, there will always be statements that

02:43.380 --> 02:48.180
cannot be proven true or false, using the rules of that system.

02:48.180 --> 02:51.760
Imagine you're playing a game, but there are moves you can't make, not because they're

02:51.760 --> 02:56.600
against the rules, but because the rules themselves can't accommodate them.

02:56.600 --> 03:01.480
In layman's terms, Gödel's theorems expose the paradox that there are truths in mathematics

03:01.480 --> 03:03.680
that cannot be proven.

03:03.680 --> 03:08.160
This is akin to knowing for certain that a locked box contains a priceless gem, but

03:08.160 --> 03:10.360
having no key to open it.

03:10.360 --> 03:15.860
The gem's existence is undeniable, yet its unreachability creates a paradox.

03:15.860 --> 03:21.040
This is not to say that mathematics is flawed or incomplete, rather, it's a testament to

03:21.040 --> 03:26.160
the richness and depth of the mathematical universe, a universe that extends beyond the

03:26.160 --> 03:29.300
horizon of what we can definitively prove.

03:29.300 --> 03:34.060
It's these paradoxes, born from the very heart of theorems, that push us to question,

03:34.060 --> 03:36.340
to explore, and to innovate.

03:36.340 --> 03:40.580
They challenge our understanding of truth and certainty, compelling us to redefine and

03:40.580 --> 03:42.340
expand our knowledge.

03:42.340 --> 03:49.100
So, paradoxes and theorems, seemingly at odds, can intertwine in fascinating ways, challenging

03:49.100 --> 03:54.380
our understanding of truth, certainty, and the very nature of knowledge itself.

03:54.380 --> 04:00.300
Remember, the world is full of wonder, especially when you dare to question the obvious.

04:00.300 --> 04:04.220
Thank you for joining us on this journey through paradoxes and theorems.

